# Spec: tracking number on the order page

## Behavior
Customers can see the carrier tracking number for a shipped order on the order page. Until the order ships, the page tells them the number is not yet available.

## Acceptance criteria
| Id   | Criterion (given / when / then) | PRD requirement | Source |
|------|----------------------------------|-----------------|--------|
| AC-1 | Given a shipped order, when the order is fetched, then `tracking_number` is a string value. | PROJ-123#R2 | J-1 |
| AC-2 | Given an order that has not shipped, when the order is fetched, then `tracking_number` is `null`. | PROJ-123#R2 | J-1 |
| AC-3 | Given a shipped order, when the order page is shown, then it prints `Tracking: <number>`. | PROJ-123#R2 | J-2 |
| AC-4 | Given a null tracking number, when the order page is shown, then it prints `Tracking: not yet available`. | PROJ-123#R2 | J-3 |
| AC-5 | Given an order without the `tracking_number` field during a rolling deployment, when the order page is shown, then it prints `Tracking: not yet available`. | PROJ-123#R2 | added |

## Traceability
| Source id | Spec AC | Status | Reason |
|-----------|---------|--------|--------|
| J-1 | AC-1, AC-2 | changed | one Jira criterion became two testable API cases |
| J-2 | AC-3 | kept | |
| J-3 | AC-4 | kept | |
| none | AC-5 | added | the web app must remain usable during the API rollout when the field is temporarily absent |

## Out of scope
Carrier links, tracking history, email notifications, and any order-page UI beyond the tracking line.

